From cf0236bff7b2831c7ec60799be608a90932192fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 01:08:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: sun50i-a64-pinephone: Fix mpu6050
 mount matrix

The mpu6050 carried the same mount matrix as the lis3mdl, but the two
chips are mounted 180 degrees apart in-plane, so the IMU device frame
came out rotated 180 degrees around z (all screen rotations detected
by userspace fusion were inverted).

Proof, from measurements on a PinePhone 1.2:

The mount matrix maps chip-frame vectors to the device frame,
v_dev = M * v_chip, with the device frame x = right screen edge,
y = top screen edge, z = out of the screen.

Raw sysfs readings (in_accel_*_raw, scale 0.000598 m/s^2/LSB;
in_magn_*_raw, scale 0.000146 G/LSB), averaged, noise < 2%:

  pose                       chip-frame reading
  ------------------------   ------------------------------------
  A) flat, screen up         accel (  408,    4, 15896)
                                 = ( 0.24, 0.00,  9.51) m/s^2
  B) upright, top edge up    accel (16700,  800,   200)
                                 = ( 9.99, 0.48,  0.12) m/s^2
  C) flat, top edge to       magn  (-4164,  152, -1227)
     magnetic north              = (-60.8,  2.2, -17.9) uT

At rest the accelerometer reads the reaction to gravity, i.e. +1 g
along whatever chip axis points up:

- pose A: up = device +z, measured along chip +z
    => M * z_chip = z_dev
- pose B: up = device +y, measured along chip +x
    => M * x_chip = y_dev
- the third column follows from the chip frame being right-handed:
    M * y_chip = M * (z_chip x x_chip) = z_dev x y_dev = -x_dev

Writing these as the columns of M:

  M = [ 0 -1  0 ]
      [ 1  0  0 ]
      [ 0  0  1 ]

which is a proper rotation (det = +1, chip rotated 90 deg around z
relative to the screen) and the transpose of the old matrix; the old
matrix has M_old * x_chip = -y_dev, i.e. it claims the phone is
upside down in pose B, and differs from the correct one by exactly
Rz(180) - matching the uniformly 180-degree-wrong rotation detection
that prompted this.

Pose C independently anchors the device-frame convention through the
lis3mdl, whose matrix is unchanged: applying it to the measurement
gives (2.2, 60.8, -17.9) uT in the device frame - horizontal
component along +y (top edge) which points north, vertical component
along -z (field dips into the ground on the northern hemisphere),
both as expected, while its chip-frame x sign is opposite to the
mpu6050's in the same physical orientation - confirming the two
chips really are mounted 180 degrees apart and need different
matrices.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi
index 3f028676b9e0..1936ac477e43 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi
@@ -702,8 +702,8 @@ accelerometer@68 {
 		interrupts = <7 5 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>; /* PH5 */
 		vdd-supply = <&reg_dldo1>;
 		vddio-supply = <&reg_dldo1>;
-		mount-matrix = "0", "1", "0",
-			       "-1", "0", "0",
+		mount-matrix = "0", "-1", "0",
+			       "1", "0", "0",
 			       "0", "0", "1";
 	};
 };
